## What You've Learnt

After completing this lesson, you can:

- ✓ Use DJNZ instruction for counted loops
- ✓ Understand B register as loop counter
- ✓ Draw multiple characters in a row
- ✓ Combine loops with character display
- ✓ Navigate screen memory horizontally (INC E)
- ✓ Understand scanline addressing for row 0
- ✓ Use PUSH/POP to preserve values across loops
- ✓ Recognize when to use DJNZ vs manual loops
- ✓ Understand screen memory "thirds" concept
- ✓ Build foundation for drawing game objects

**Key insight:** Loops transform repetitive tasks from impossible to trivial. One instruction (DJNZ) replaces hundreds of lines. This is how you draw snake bodies, alien grids, and scrolling text.

**Important discovery:** Screen memory addressing is more complex than it first appears. Row 0 works simply with INC D, but other rows require understanding the "thirds" structure. Start simple, master the pattern, then tackle complexity.

**Next lesson:** Clearing Up - Learn the XOR A trick to quickly set A=0, then use loops to clear entire screen. Foundation for screen management and game resets!

